+  <div class="wrap">
+    <h2>What a management company actually does, and what software covers</h2>
+    <p>The honest version of this list is the whole decision. Software now runs the office half of the job. The physical half (showings, inspections, being reachable) stays with you or a per-task vendor.</p>
+    <div class="tbl-scroll"><table class="cmp">
+      <tr><th>Task</th><th>Can software do it?</th></tr>
+      <tr><td>Advertise and list vacant units (syndication to Zillow, Apartments.com, etc.)</td><td class="">Mostly - software pushes one listing to the major portals automatically; photos and pricing judgment remain human</td></tr><tr><td>Schedule and run showings</td><td class="">Partially - scheduling tools and self-showing lockboxes handle booking; someone local still opens doors or checks the property after</td></tr><tr><td>Tenant screening (credit, criminal, eviction history, income verification)</td><td class="">Mostly - TransUnion SmartMove-class reports run instantly inside software; the accept/reject decision and consistent criteria are the owner&#039;s</td></tr><tr><td>Lease preparation and signing</td><td class="">Mostly - state-specific templates plus e-signature; a local attorney review of the base lease is a one-time cost, not a monthly one</td></tr><tr><td>Move-in / move-out inspections and condition documentation</td><td class="bad">No - requires physical presence; apps only standardize the checklist and photo record</td></tr><tr><td>Rent collection</td><td class="good">Fully - ACH/card autopay, tenant portal, automatic receipts and ledger posting; 78-86% of renters prefer paying online anyway (Buildium/AppFolio)</td></tr><tr><td>Late-payment follow-up, reminders, and late fees</td><td class="good">Fully - automatic reminders before due date, auto-assessed late fees, escalating notices without the owner touching it</td></tr><tr><td>Bookkeeping, owner statements, and tax-time reporting</td><td class="">Mostly - every payment and expense posts to the ledger automatically with Schedule E-ready exports; a CPA still files</td></tr><tr><td>Maintenance request intake and dispatch</td><td class="">Mostly - tenants submit requests with photos through a portal, software routes to the assigned vendor and tracks status; the owner still needs vendor relationships and approves big-ticket work</td></tr><tr><td>24/7 emergency response</td><td class="">Partially - intake, triage rules, and vendor routing are automatable; a human plumber still has to show up at 2am</td></tr><tr><td>Vendor management (COIs, invoices, 1099s)</td><td class="">Partially - software tracks documents, invoices, and issues 1099s; finding and keeping good vendors is human work</td></tr><tr><td>Routine property inspections / drive-bys</td><td class="bad">No - physical task; software only schedules and documents it</td></tr><tr><td>Lease renewals and rent increases</td><td class="">Mostly - renewal offers, market-rent comps, and e-sign renewals are automated; the pricing call is the owner&#039;s</td></tr><tr><td>Security deposit handling and itemized deductions</td><td class="">Mostly - escrow tracking, deadlines, and itemization letters are automated; state-specific rules still need to be set correctly once</td></tr><tr><td>Evictions and court appearances</td><td class="bad">No - attorney and court process; software&#039;s contribution is a clean payment ledger and notice trail that wins cases</td></tr><tr><td>Fair housing / legal compliance</td><td class="">Partially - software enforces identical screening criteria and documented processes, which is most of fair-housing defense; knowing local law is not automatable</td></tr>
+    </table></div>
+  </div>
+</section><section>
+  <div class="wrap">
+    <h2>Where the rent sits each month</h2>
+    <p>With a management company, rent lands in the manager's trust account. You get the remainder after fees on their statement schedule, usually mid-month or later, and repair costs come out before you see them, with any markup already applied. Most owner complaints about managers are some version of this paragraph: slow statements, unclear deductions, surprises at payout time.</p>
+    <p>Self-managing with software inverts that. Rent goes from the tenant's bank to yours through the payment processor, every charge and payment sits in a ledger you can open any hour, and repair invoices are numbers you approved rather than line items you discover. RenPro's books update with every payment, and the built-in AI answers questions about any number on the statement because it reads the same ledger.</p>
+  </div>
+</section><section>
+  <div class="wrap">
+    <h2>When hiring a Myrtle Beach management company still makes sense</h2>
+    <p>This page argues for self-managing with software because the math usually favors it. Not always. The honest exceptions:</p>
+    <ul class="checks"><li>Out-of-state and remote owners: maintenance and emergency response is the single hardest thing to do from hundreds of miles away; a local manager provides around-the-clock physical response and an existing vendor network (Redfin, &#039;Managing a Rental Property Out of State&#039;; Baselane, &#039;How to Manage Out-of-State Rental Property&#039;). For short-term rentals managed from out of state, the guidance is that hiring a manager is almost always advisable despite 25-40% fees.</li><li>Scale: the Census/HUD 2021 Rental Housing Finance Survey shows professional management climbing from ~22% of 1-4 unit properties to 84% of 150+ unit properties - past a certain door count you are hiring management labor either way, whether it&#039;s a PM firm or your own staff.</li><li>Eviction-heavy or heavily regulated markets: an average eviction runs $3,500 and can reach $10,000+ (TransUnion SmartMove; Snappt), and procedural mistakes restart the clock and burn additional months of rent (~$2,540 average lost rent). A PM firm with an attorney pipeline and process discipline earns its fee if evictions are a recurring reality in your market.</li><li>Owners with no time or high-value time: Census-derived data (iPropertyManagement) shows roughly 1 in 6 landlord-managed properties consumes 20-79 hours/month. If your property is a high-touch building or your working hour is worth more than the fee, 8-12% is a rational trade.</li><li>Honest caveat that cuts both ways: UK survey data (Pegasus Insight, 2026) found landlords&#039; reported time commitment was broadly similar whether or not they used an agent - hiring a PM buys execution, not zero involvement; the owner still oversees, approves, and audits.</li></ul>
+  </div>
+</section><section id="renpro">
+  <div class="wrap">
+    <h2>The software alternative at a flat rate</h2>
+    <p>RenPro is the flat-rate back office for rental portfolios: automated rent collection, AI late-payment follow-up, maintenance, owner payouts, and reporting. One price, everything included, up to 150 units. It was built by a 15-year operator who runs his own 500+ door portfolio on it.</p>
+    <div class="twocol">
+      <div class="panel">
+        <h3 style="margin-top:0">Everything included at one price</h3>
+        <ul class="checks"><li>Automated rent collection with tenant portal and autopay enrollment</li><li>AI manager built in: ask your portfolio anything, it answers from your real data</li><li>AI late-payment follow-up that drafts and sends reminders</li><li>Maintenance work orders with vendor dispatch</li><li>Owner portal, owner statements, and owner payouts</li><li>Unified messaging: web, email, and text from one inbox</li><li>Applicant screening and online applications</li><li>Financial reporting and delinquency tracking</li><li>Live in 2 minutes, no onboarding fee, no implementation call required</li></ul>
+      </div>
+      <div class="panel">
