x Familial Status Disability Fair Housing Enforcement Secretary Scott Turnerâ€™s mission is to deliver affordable, accessible housing for the American people. Prior FHEO enforcement undermined this mission by strongarming housing providers, appraisers, and localities to adopt measures beyond the scope of the Fair Housing Act. Prior enforcement actions were driven by FHEO guidance that prioritized â€œappraisal biasâ€ cases, controlling local land use, and other radical policies like discouraging criminal background checks for prospective tenants and encouraging illegal aliens to file complaints consequently making housing less affordable, less accessible, and less safe for the most vulnerable Americans. This will end under Secretary Turnerâ€™s leadership. HUD is eliminating artificial, arbitrary, and unnecessary barriers to housing by restoring fair housing enforcement to its core statutory mission. New guidance reflects this vision by: Prioritizing cases with actual, provable instances of discrimination Ending illegal discrimination and ensuring all Americans can exercise fair housing rights Rescinding guidance that stifled economic innovation and burdened private enterprise Defending due process by reining in regulatory overreach See FHEO guidance documents below: Fair Housing Act Enforcement and Prioritization of Resources This guidance outlines FHEOâ€™s role under the United States Constitution and Fair Housing Act; explains why FHEO must prioritize resources for cases with strong evidence of intentional discrimination; and articulates how future enforcement efforts will proceed. Notice of Withdrawal of FHEO Guidance Documents This notice informs the public, including the Departmentâ€™s stakeholders, of the guidance documents that the Department is withdrawing through an exercise of the Departmentâ€™s policy-making discretion Notice of Letter Rescinding Operations Allies Welcome Guidance The Office of Fair Housing and Equal Opportunity has rescinded Biden-era guidance that endorsed illegal preferential treatment for Afghan Nationals over native-born Americans. The rescinded guidance, issued under the Biden Administrationâ€™s â€œOperation Allies Welcomeâ€, under the guise of instructing housing providers about their obligations under the Fair Housing Act, instead created special housing exemptions for immigrants and refugeesâ€”but not American citizens. Through this new guidance, HUD is furthering the Fair Housing Actâ€™s promise of equal opportunity, ensuring that we always prioritize the American people. Additional Resources File a Complaint Helping Americans HUD Partners Researchers News About Contact U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development 451 7th Street, S.W., Washington, DC 20410 T: (202) 402-3815 Find a HUD office near you Search Office of Inspector General | No Fear Act | FOIA | Privacy | Accessibility | Web Policies | Sitemap 
